WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.709
Diffusion runs a large network at every
step.

2
00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:07.789
Neighbouring steps compute almost the same
thing.

3
00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:13.581
Caching stores one feature and reuses it
later, skipping the repeated work.

4
00:00:13.831 --> 00:00:18.826
The question is how far one stored feature
can be pushed.

5
00:00:19.426 --> 00:00:23.991
The cached feature is held while the true
feature moves.

6
00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:28.620
Skip one step, and the error is small.

7
00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:33.127
Skip many steps, and the stale feature
drifts away.

8
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:36.470
Errors accumulate, and quality degrades.

9
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.549
Reuse is cheap, but stale features become
wrong.

10
00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:45.714
Most caching methods predict the future
feature from the past.

11
00:00:46.514 --> 00:00:48.937
LearniBridge asks a different question.

12
00:00:49.187 --> 00:00:54.330
What correction makes a cached computation
behave like the skipped full computation?

13
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:59.146
Cached computation plus a small correction
approximates the future one.

14
00:01:00.146 --> 00:01:04.831
Stack layer inputs from many samples, one
column per sample.

15
00:01:05.631 --> 00:01:09.340
The correction is the layer difference
between timesteps.

16
00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.299
Model it as a matrix times those inputs.

17
00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:18.114
The best matrix is E times the
pseudo-inverse of X.

18
00:01:18.364 --> 00:01:23.639
Its singular values decay fast: energy
sits in a few directions.

19
00:01:24.439 --> 00:01:27.719
The required correction is therefore low
rank.

20
00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:31.599
Split one hundred prompts into disjoint
groups.

21
00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:35.250
Fit the optimal correction per group.

22
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.351
Each has its own principal subspace.

23
00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:41.024
Measure the angles between them.

24
00:01:41.824 --> 00:01:44.247
They come out consistently small.

25
00:01:44.497 --> 00:01:47.777
So a few calibration prompts can
generalize.

26
00:01:48.377 --> 00:01:51.229
Look at the last Transformer block.

27
00:01:52.029 --> 00:01:54.451
Keep the pretrained weights frozen.

28
00:01:54.701 --> 00:01:58.530
Add a thin low-rank path, LoRA (low-rank
adaptation).

29
00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.489
The cached input goes through this
augmented block.

30
00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:05.590
It approximates the skipped full
computation.

31
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.691
Low rank follows from the analysis.

32
00:02:09.291 --> 00:02:12.143
Calibration runs a few full trajectories.

33
00:02:12.943 --> 00:02:19.746
Record the final-block input at a computed
step, and the true output at a skipped

34
00:02:19.746 --> 00:02:20.200
one.

35
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.587
Train only the LoRA weights to connect the
two.

36
00:02:24.837 --> 00:02:29.951
At inference, compute fully every N steps
and cache that input.

37
00:02:30.201 --> 00:02:33.601
In between, skip the earlier blocks
entirely.

38
00:02:34.401 --> 00:02:38.350
Only the final block, with its adapter,
runs.

39
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.646
Stale cache, structured correction, low
rank, few prompts, lightweight bridge.
